---
import FocusedOfferLayout from '../layouts/FocusedOfferLayout.astro';
import ExampleFinding from '../components/ExampleFinding.astro';
import type { Faq } from '../lib/schema';
const faq: Faq[] = [
  { q: 'What should I send for a slow query review?', a: 'The query text with sensitive literals replaced, EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) output or a plain EXPLAIN if running it is costly, the table definitions and indexes involved, approximate row counts, and the parameter values that are slow.' },
  { q: 'Is it safe to run EXPLAIN ANALYZE on production?', a: 'EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the statement. For a SELECT that means the full cost of the query. For INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE, run it inside a transaction you roll back, or send plain EXPLAIN and an existing auto_explain log entry instead.' },
  { q: 'Can you review a query generated by an ORM?', a: 'Yes. Send the SQL the ORM produced and the code that calls it. The fix may be an index, a different query shape or an ORM option such as eager loading, and the report says which one to test first.' },
  { q: 'Do you rewrite the query for me?', a: 'I propose ranked changes with the SQL for each and a before-and-after check. Your team tests and ships them. The scope is one statement and one clarification round, not unlimited rewrites.' },
  { q: 'When is a single query review the wrong choice?', a: 'When several endpoints slow down together, locks pass between sessions, or you cannot yet name the statement. Those need the full PostgreSQL performance audit, which ranks the whole workload.' }
];
---
<FocusedOfferLayout service="query-rescue" title="PostgreSQL Slow Query Optimization & Plan Review | WalStack" eyebrow="Query Rescue · one statement" heading="PostgreSQL slow query optimization and execution plan review" faq={faq}
  description="Get one slow PostgreSQL query and its execution plan reviewed: written findings, ranked fixes with lock risk, and a before-and-after validation plan."
  headline="One slow query. A clear diagnosis. A safer next step."
  fit="For a backend engineer or small SaaS team that can name the statement behind a slow request, job or report, including SQL generated by an ORM. I review the query in its execution context and explain which change is worth testing first."
  excludes="The scope excludes unlimited rewrites, production deployment, incident response and 24/7 coverage. Runtime improvement depends on your data and workload.">
  <Fragment slot="inputs"><p>Start with a short description, engine and version, observed latency and frequency. Mention whether the slowdown follows a release, data growth or a different parameter value.</p>
    <p>After scope agreement, share redacted SQL, relevant schema and indexes, row counts and a representative plan if available. Keep proprietary code and plan files out of the public form.</p></Fragment>
  <p slot="result">The report identifies the plan step or wait that best explains the symptom, the limits of the evidence and the comparable inputs to use when testing.</p>
  <p>Query Rescue starts from a statement you can already name, so it does not need the full-cluster snapshot. If you don't yet know which statement is responsible, start with the <a href="/snapshot">free PostgreSQL snapshot</a> or the <a href="/postgresql-performance-investigation">Postgres Slowdown Investigation</a> instead.</p>
  <h2>Example finding</h2>
  <ExampleFinding label="From a lab on generated data. Not a client result."
    symptom="Deleting one customer takes seconds, and the time grows with the size of the orders table."
    cause="orders.customer_id references customers, but has no index. For every deleted customer, PostgreSQL scans orders to check the foreign key."
    fix={`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_id_idx
  ON orders (customer_id);`}
    lockRisk="CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY takes SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE, so reads and writes continue. It waits for transactions already open on the table, and a failed build leaves an INVALID index to drop before retrying."
    validation="EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) on the DELETE shows the foreign-key trigger time drop. pg_stat_user_indexes shows the new index being scanned."
    source={{ href: '/insights/postgresql-foreign-key-index-slow-delete', label: 'The full foreign-key lab' }} />
  <h2>Which evidence fits your database?</h2>
  <h3>PostgreSQL</h3><p>An existing <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> plan can show where estimates diverge or a sort spills. If executing the query is costly or changes data, start with plain <code>EXPLAIN</code> or a prior capture. The <a href="/insights/how-to-profile-slow-postgresql-queries">slow-query profiling guide</a> explains safe collection options.</p>
  <h3>SQL Server</h3><p>Query Store can show when a different plan appeared and how its runtime statistics changed. An existing actual execution plan adds runtime row counts. Include representative parameter types and whether latency varies by value. See the <a href="/insights/sql-server-parameter-sniffing-query-store">parameter-sensitivity lab</a>.</p>
  <h3>When the bottleneck spans queries</h3><p>If several endpoints slow down together, locks move between sessions or the dominant statement is unknown, scope a <a href="/postgresql-performance-investigation">PostgreSQL Performance Investigation</a> or <a href="/sql-server-performance">SQL Server Performance Review</a>.</p>
</FocusedOfferLayout>
